1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,567
Before a buyer can work with a new supplier, someone needs to know whether

2
00:00:04,567 --> 00:00:07,830
the business details and supporting proof describe the same entity.

3
00:00:07,830 --> 00:00:12,397
When they do not, the supplier and reviewer both need a clear next step.

4
00:00:12,397 --> 00:00:15,334
This demo follows that decision from submission through resolution.

5
00:00:17,016 --> 00:00:22,128
Here is Apex Components, a fictional practice supplier. The demo fill uses only details

6
00:00:22,128 --> 00:00:27,240
linked to this account; the vendor still reviews the business information, required documents and

7
00:00:27,240 --> 00:00:32,352
legal declarations before submitting. Watch the document reading and validation stages run after the

8
00:00:32,352 --> 00:00:36,003
click. The application reference and processing result are then saved.

9
00:00:36,003 --> 00:00:41,115
A supplier can return to see what happened, while the buyer can inspect the

10
00:00:41,115 --> 00:00:46,227
evidence behind the outcome. The form is an entry point into a real review

11
00:00:46,227 --> 00:00:48,783
workflow, rather than a decorative success screen.

12
00:01:03,032 --> 00:01:08,105
The procurement desk sees the new application alongside the rest of its organisation's queue.

13
00:01:08,105 --> 00:01:13,178
Each case shows its status, priority and next action, so staff can quickly work

14
00:01:13,178 --> 00:01:17,164
out what needs attention. Procurement assigns Rajesh Iyer as the reviewer.

15
00:01:17,164 --> 00:01:22,237
That creates an accountable handoff and makes the case visible in his review desk.

16
00:01:22,237 --> 00:01:26,947
Procurement coordinates the work, while the final approval is reserved for a reviewer.

17
00:01:29,047 --> 00:01:34,116
Rajesh opens a case assigned to him. The case brings the submitted business details,

18
00:01:34,116 --> 00:01:39,185
uploaded proof, automated findings and decision controls together. He can trace why a check

19
00:01:39,185 --> 00:01:43,892
passed or failed before making a judgment. A green check is useful preparation;

20
00:01:43,892 --> 00:01:48,961
it is not an autonomous approval. For this clean supplier, the documents and declared

21
00:01:48,961 --> 00:01:54,030
details are consistent. Rajesh records an approval with a reason, and the decision becomes

22
00:01:54,030 --> 00:01:58,013
part of the activity trail. The same controls would block approval

23
00:01:58,013 --> 00:02:00,185
while a serious issue remains open.

24
00:02:05,047 --> 00:02:09,698
The vendor sees the approval on its own dashboard and receives an in-app notice

25
00:02:09,698 --> 00:02:14,348
that opens the decision receipt. The receipt includes the application reference and decision date,

26
00:02:14,348 --> 00:02:18,999
and says there are no remaining application tasks. That closes the loop for the

27
00:02:18,999 --> 00:02:22,321
supplier instead of leaving them staring at a submitted status.

28
00:02:22,321 --> 00:02:24,979
Approval here means the onboarding review is complete;

29
00:02:24,979 --> 00:02:28,301
it does not claim to activate purchasing access or payments.

30
00:02:28,813 --> 00:02:33,730
Now watch a second prepared draft being submitted live. It sits in a separate

31
00:02:33,730 --> 00:02:37,594
fictional buyer workspace, so there is no unrelated duplicate supplier signal.

32
00:02:37,594 --> 00:02:42,511
The company PAN looks plausible. The GST number also looks plausible at a glance.

33
00:02:42,511 --> 00:02:47,429
But a GST number contains the taxpayer PAN, and this one embeds a different

34
00:02:47,429 --> 00:02:51,292
company's PAN. The draft has no validation result before this submission.

35
00:02:51,292 --> 00:02:56,210
When the vendor clicks Submit, Zamp runs the checks, saves the failed cross-field finding

36
00:02:56,210 --> 00:03:01,127
and holds approval. The case tells the vendor to confirm which entity's credentials were

37
00:03:01,127 --> 00:03:06,044
supplied. This is more useful than rejecting a field just because its format looks

38
00:03:06,044 --> 00:03:10,962
wrong: both fields have the right shape, yet together they tell a contradictory story.

39
00:03:10,962 --> 00:03:14,123
A reviewer can see the issue and the evidence

40
00:03:14,123 --> 00:03:16,230
without hunting through a technical log.

41
00:03:31,813 --> 00:03:35,358
The vendor can respond in the same place as the request.

42
00:03:35,358 --> 00:03:39,869
Here they enter the GST number that matches the declared company PAN and explain

43
00:03:39,869 --> 00:03:43,092
the correction. A new check runs against the revised value.

44
00:03:43,092 --> 00:03:47,604
The reviewer queue then marks the case as having a supplier reply, and the

45
00:03:47,604 --> 00:03:52,115
response is visible inside the case. The previous failed run stays in history, so

46
00:03:52,115 --> 00:03:56,627
the change can be understood later. This reply does not silently turn into an

47
00:03:56,627 --> 00:04:00,816
approval: the assigned reviewer must assess the new evidence and record the decision.

48
00:04:05,829 --> 00:04:10,215
Processing Runs keeps the conflict result and the check that produced it.

49
00:04:10,215 --> 00:04:15,332
A separate prepared case shows a registry timeout. That is unknown evidence, rather than

50
00:04:15,332 --> 00:04:19,717
proof the vendor is wrong, so staff should retry or review it.

51
00:04:19,717 --> 00:04:23,372
The supplier is not asked to repair a registry outage.

52
00:04:23,372 --> 00:04:27,027
Every document and registry response in this recording is synthetic.

53
00:04:27,027 --> 00:04:32,144
In a pilot, I would measure decision time, unsafe approvals, and valid suppliers held

54
00:04:32,144 --> 00:04:36,529
unnecessarily. The goal is a faster, clearer decision with a defensible record.
